\title{Univariate polynomials with vector coefficients}
\taxon{Definition}
\tag{definition}
\meta{defines}{vector-valued univariate polynomial; polynomial with vector coefficients; coefficient tuple}
\meta{dependencies}{None.}
\meta{provenance}{Notation used in [OpenAI, 23 September 2026](cn-0042), proof of Lemma 2.1; explanatory definition added on 9 October 2026.}

\p{Let #{Z} be a real vector space. A \em{univariate polynomial with coefficients in #{Z}} is a finite formal sum}

##{P(\xi)=\sum_{r=0}^{h}z_r\xi^r,\qquad z_r\in Z.}

\p{There is one indeterminate, #{\xi}, and each coefficient #{z_r} is a vector in #{Z}. The notation #{Z[\xi]} denotes the space of all such polynomials. For #{P\ne0}, its degree is the largest #{r} with #{z_r\ne0}; the zero polynomial is included in every degree bound. Evaluation at a real number #{\zeta} uses scalar multiplication in #{Z}:}

##{P(\zeta)=\sum_{r=0}^{h}\zeta^r z_r\in Z.}

\p{For degree at most #{h}, the \em{coefficient tuple} is #{v=(z_0,\ldots,z_h)\in Z^{h+1}}. With the monomial vector #{\widehat\zeta=(1,\zeta,\ldots,\zeta^h)\in\mathbb R^{h+1}}, one can write #{P(\zeta)=\langle v,\widehat\zeta\rangle} using the bilinear pairing}

##{\langle(z_0,\ldots,z_h),(a_0,\ldots,a_h)\rangle=\sum_{r=0}^{h}a_rz_r\in Z.}

\p{This pairing takes values in #{Z}; it requires only the vector-space operations on #{Z}. Thus the whole coefficient tuple belongs to #{Z^{h+1}}, while each individual coefficient and each evaluated value belong to #{Z}.}

\p{If #{e_1,\ldots,e_d} is a basis of #{Z}, equivalently #{P(\xi)=\sum_{j=1}^{d}p_j(\xi)e_j} with scalar univariate polynomials #{p_j\in\mathbb R[\xi]}. There are #{d=\dim Z} coordinate polynomials; their common degree bound #{h} is independent of #{d}. In these coordinates the coefficient tuple is a #{d\times(h+1)} real matrix, which multiplies the column monomial vector to give the coordinates of #{P(\zeta)}.}

\p{For example, with #{Z=\mathbb R^2},}

##{P(\xi)=(1,3)+\xi(2,0)+\xi^2(0,-1)=(1+2\xi,\,3-\xi^2).}

\p{This is a degree-two polynomial in one variable, with values in #{\mathbb R^2}; for instance #{P(1)=(3,2)}.}
